<Project>

  <!--
    Central Package Management for CBIX.
    Every PackageReference version lives here; .csproj files carry the package
    identity only. Design §11 requires exact pinned versions (MAF and its Anthropic
    integration are young and prerelease) - so no floating or wildcard versions.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <ManagePackageVersionsCentrally>true</ManagePackageVersionsCentrally>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup Label="Runtime">
    <!-- The LLM-neutral currency of the whole solution (design 3): AIAgent/IChatClient and
         the AIContent hierarchy live here, and MAF builds on them. Cbix.Core depends on
         these abstractions ONLY - never on a provider package - so a provider swap stays a
         configuration change. Provider-specific payloads ride inside a profile via
         AIContent.RawRepresentation, never in a Core signature. -->
    <PackageVersion Include="Microsoft.Extensions.AI.Abstractions" Version="10.9.0" />
    <PackageVersion Include="Microsoft.Extensions.Hosting" Version="10.0.10" />
    <!-- DI abstractions only - IServiceCollection, IServiceProvider, ServiceDescriptor and the
         TryAdd/keyed-service helpers. Cbix.Core needs it because CLAUDE.md puts workflow-graph
         composition there (story S01-13): describing the graph's registrations means naming
         IServiceCollection, while the container implementation, the host and the configuration
         binding all stay on the host's side of the split.

         Neutral by the same test the other Core dependencies pass: it names no model provider and
         makes no network call, which is why adding it to CoreAssemblyNeutralityTests' allowlist is
         a deliberate, commented act. A container IMPLEMENTATION would still not belong in Core.

         Version matches the Hosting pin above rather than the 10.0.9 the MAF Workflows package
         asks for: they resolve to one assembly in the graph regardless, and a Core pin BELOW the
         host's would be a downgrade nobody chose. It arrives transitively either way; declaring it
         is what makes the version a decision instead of an outcome. -->
    <PackageVersion Include="Microsoft.Extensions.DependencyInjection.Abstractions" Version="10.0.10" />
    <!-- Logging abstractions only - ILogger/ILogger<T>, no sink, no provider. Cbix.Core needs it
         because a security refusal that is only an exception is invisible until something catches
         it: the ingest containment gate has to emit a structured event at the moment it refuses.
         Neutral by construction (it names no model provider), which is why adding it to
         CoreAssemblyNeutralityTests' allowlist is a deliberate, commented act rather than a
         loophole. -->
    <PackageVersion Include="Microsoft.Extensions.Logging.Abstractions" Version="10.0.10" />
    <!-- The local PDF text layer (design 5.1), and the corpus the validator's grounding gate
         does verbatim string containment against (design 5.6). Latest STABLE line: 0.1.15,
         released 2026-06-25; the 0.1.16-alpha-* stream on nuget.org is the project's rolling
         CI feed and is not a release.

         Why this belongs in Cbix.Core and does not weaken LLM-agnosticism: PdfPig is a local,
         offline, Apache-2.0 PDF parsing library. It is a neutral document library, not a
         provider SDK - it names no model provider, makes no network call, and is the same
         dependency under every provider profile (the Claude native-PDF profile, the generic
         vision profile and the text-only fallback all need this text layer). Adding it to
         CoreAssemblyNeutralityTests' allowlist is therefore the deliberate act that list's
         procedure describes, not a loophole.

         Transitive closure: NONE on our target framework. PdfPig 0.1.15's nuspec declares
         dependencies only for its .NET Framework 4.6.2/4.7.1 and netstandard2.0 groups
         (Microsoft.Bcl.HashCode, System.Memory, System.ValueTuple - all polyfills); the
         net6.0 and net8.0 groups are empty, and net10.0 resolves the net8.0 group. The
         package does ship seven assemblies in one lib folder (UglyToad.PdfPig plus .Core,
         .DocumentLayoutAnalysis, .Fonts, .Package, .Tokenization, .Tokens), so the
         neutrality allowlist names the ones Core actually emits a reference to, not the
         package id.

         No bracket pin, and no claim that the version is safe because the package is
         "stable": it is 0.1.x, and a 0.x scheme explicitly disclaims a stability promise.
         What actually holds this version still is mechanical - the committed
         packages.lock.json plus CI's locked restore, which fail the build if the resolved
         version moves for any reason.

         Upgrade procedure, and it is not a formality: this package IS the grounding corpus.
         A PdfPig release that changes reading order, word-gap inference or whitespace by so
         much as one character changes the text every SourceSnippet is checked against, and
         that drift is invisible in a build, a unit test and a diff alike - it surfaces only
         as grounding-gate failures on documents that used to pass. So a version bump
         requires a green golden-set run before it ships (CLAUDE.md quality gate), and until
         the eval harness exists there is no way to see the damage at all. Bump deliberately
         or not at all. -->
    <PackageVersion Include="PdfPig" Version="0.1.15" />
    <!-- Page rasterisation for the generic-vision document-content profile (design 5.1, story
         S01-06). PDFPig parses PDFs but cannot render them, and the agnosticism fallback has to
         send page IMAGES: the permission matrix is a visual table, and a provider without a native
         PDF mode reads it only if something locally turns each page into pixels.

         Why PDFtoImage, with the evidence the choice was made on (all measured 2026-08-16 against
         nuget.org, not recalled):

           - Renderer quality, which is the actual driver. It wraps PDFium - the renderer Chrome
             ships - so table rules, cell alignment and small type come out the way a human sees
             them in a viewer. Sprint 02's Matrix agent has to read 8-10pt cells off these images;
             a renderer with layout gaps would be paid for in matrix cell accuracy, the PoC's
             headline metric.
           - Supply chain, which is why 5.3.0 and not 5.4.0. PDFtoImage is AUTHOR-signed
             (CN="Open Source Developer, David Sungaila", Certum) as well as repository-signed by
             nuget.org - measured with `dotnet nuget verify` in all-checks mode (the double-dash
             flag is spelled out rather than written literally: two consecutive hyphens end an XML
             comment, and MSBuild reports it as every package having no version) - a stronger posture
             than several packages already in this graph (Reqnroll is repository-signed only). It
             is MIT, 6.66M downloads, and its source repo is named in the nuspec with a commit sha.
             5.4.0 was published 2026-08-16T19:29Z, hours before this pin was chosen; 5.3.0 was
             published 2026-07-28. A zero-soak release is precisely the window in which a
             compromised-maintainer package is live and unreported, and nothing in 5.4.0's release
             notes is needed here, so the soaked version is the one taken.
           - Linux CI, verified rather than assumed. `runs-on: ubuntu-latest`, so a rasteriser whose
             native library will not load there is a broken gate, not an inconvenience. Measured in
             a linux-x64 container against the real DE specimen: the render produces PNGs (see the
             note on PdfiumPageImageRenderer). bblanchon.PDFium.Linux ships
             runtimes/linux-x64/native/libpdfium.so, and PDFtoImage takes
             SkiaSharp.NativeAssets.Linux.NoDependencies - the self-contained variant that does not
             need libfontconfig on the image.

         Transitive closure on net10.0 (from the 5.3.0 nuspec's net10.0 group, which exists - this
         is not a package resolving an older group by fallback): SkiaSharp 4.150.1 plus its
         Win32/macOS/Linux.NoDependencies native asset packages, and bblanchon.PDFium
         Win32/macOS/Linux 152.0.7961. The SkiaSharp packages are author-signed by Microsoft
         Corporation.

         ACCEPTED RESIDUAL RISK, stated rather than glossed: bblanchon.PDFium.* is
         repository-signed only - no author signature - and it is the package that carries the
         native code. Provenance is otherwise good (Apache-2.0, built in public CI from Google's
         pdfium sources at a nuspec-named commit, 8.10M downloads on the Linux package alone), and
         the mitigations are the same ones this repo already relies on for Reqnroll: an exact pin,
         a committed lock file, locked-mode restore in CI, and a single pinned feed.

         Bracket notation, deliberately - the second use in this file after the MAF pins. A bare
         version in NuGet is a MINIMUM, so a developer's unlocked local restore would quietly float
         this to 5.4.0 and undo the soak decision above, which is the entire point of choosing N-1.
         The bracket makes "we took the soaked build" a fact about every restore rather than about
         CI's.

         Why this does not weaken LLM-agnosticism, by the same test PdfPig passes: PDFium and Skia
         are local, offline rendering libraries. They name no model provider, make no network call,
         and are needed identically under any vision-capable provider - the profile that uses them
         exists precisely to work when no provider SDK is available. Extending
         CoreAssemblyNeutralityTests' allowlist for them is the deliberate act that list's procedure
         describes.

         Upgrade procedure: PDFium rendering is an input to matrix extraction, so a version bump
         changes the pixels the Matrix agent reads. Bump deliberately, and require a green
         golden-set run before it ships (CLAUDE.md quality gate). -->
    <PackageVersion Include="PDFtoImage" Version="[5.3.0]" />
    <!-- Referenced DIRECTLY rather than left to arrive through PDFtoImage, by the same rule the MAF
         pins below state: a type named in our own code should not arrive by accident. It IS named
         in our own code, unavoidably - PDFtoImage's single-parse rendering entry point
         (`ToImagesAsync`) yields `SkiaSharp.SKBitmap`, and PNG encoding goes through `SKData` and
         `SKEncodedImageFormat`. The single-parse API is a security requirement, not a preference
         (the per-page `SavePng` overload re-parses the whole document per page - quadratic work on
         attacker-controlled input), so naming these types is the price of that fix and
         CoreAssemblyNeutralityTests' allowlist gains SkiaSharp deliberately.

         Neutral by the same test PdfPig and PDFtoImage pass: a local, offline 2D graphics library
         that names no model provider and makes no network call. Author-signed by Microsoft
         Corporation as well as repository-signed (measured with `dotnet nuget verify`, all-mode),
         MIT, 313M downloads. Note the prose there rather than the flag spelling: a literal double
         hyphen terminates an XML comment, and MSBuild's report of that is every package in this
         file appearing to have no version (NU1015), which names nothing about the real cause. It
         has now cost two debugging detours in this file; write the flag out in words.

         Version 4.150.1 is exactly what PDFtoImage 5.3.0's net10.0 dependency group resolves, and
         the two MUST move together: a mismatch between the managed SkiaSharp assembly and the
         native libSkiaSharp the asset packages ship is a load failure at render time, not a
         compile error. The bracket pin makes that lockstep mechanical - bump PDFtoImage and this
         restore fails loudly until this pin is moved to match, which is the intended behaviour. -->
    <PackageVersion Include="SkiaSharp" Version="[4.150.1]" />
    <!-- MAF's workflow engine (design 5.2), added for story S01-13. This is the package that
         declares WorkflowBuilder, Executor<TIn,TOut>, ExecutorBinding, the edge model and
         InProcessExecution - i.e. the graph itself - and Cbix.Core references it, which is the
         first time Core takes a Microsoft.Agents.AI.* dependency at all.

         Why that does not weaken LLM-agnosticism, by the same test PdfPig and PDFtoImage pass:
         Microsoft.Agents.AI.Workflows names no model provider. Its whole vocabulary is graph
         vocabulary - executors, edges, supersteps, checkpoints - plus AIAgent, which IS the
         neutral currency design 3 says the solution speaks. It is the same dependency under every
         provider profile, and the composition Core builds with it takes AIAgent instances as
         input, so the HOST still decides which provider supplies them. The neutrality allowlist in
         CoreAssemblyNeutralityTests gains the neutral MAF assemblies deliberately and with that
         reasoning recorded there; Microsoft.Agents.AI.Anthropic and the Anthropic SDK stay banned
         from every non-adapter assembly, enforced separately by ProviderContainmentTests.

         Version, and why it is NOT prerelease: 1.17.0 is the stable line, matching the
         Microsoft.Agents.AI / .Abstractions pins below exactly. Its net10.0 dependency group
         requires Microsoft.Agents.AI 1.17.0 and Microsoft.Agents.AI.Abstractions 1.17.0 (measured
         against the published nuspec, 2026-08-17), so the three move as a set - bump one and the
         locked restore fails until the others follow, which is the intended behaviour.

         Bracket notation for the same reason the pins below carry it: design 11 requires exact
         pinned versions for MAF, and a bare version in NuGet is a MINIMUM.

         TRANSITIVE CLOSURE, stated rather than glossed because it is the largest this file adds and
         it lands in Core. Read from the REGENERATED LOCK FILE rather than from the nuspec's declared
         dependency group - an earlier version of this comment was written from the nuspec and was
         wrong by five packages, because a nuspec lists direct dependencies while the closure is what
         resolution actually produces. The full set Cbix.Core's lock gained:

           Microsoft.Agents.AI, Microsoft.Agents.AI.Abstractions, Microsoft.Agents.AI.Workflows
           Microsoft.Extensions.AI, Microsoft.Extensions.AI.Evaluation
           Microsoft.Extensions.Caching.Abstractions
           Microsoft.Extensions.Compliance.Abstractions
           Microsoft.Extensions.DependencyInjection.Abstractions
           Microsoft.Extensions.FileSystemGlobbing
           Microsoft.Extensions.ObjectPool
           Microsoft.Extensions.Primitives
           Microsoft.Extensions.VectorData.Abstractions
           Microsoft.ML.Tokenizers
           OpenTelemetry.Api
           System.Numerics.Tensors
           Google.Protobuf 3.30.2

         Publishers, corrected: this is NOT "all Microsoft- or CNCF-published". Google.Protobuf is
         published and author-signed by Google LLC, and it arrives through Microsoft.ML.Tokenizers
         2.0.0 (which declares Google.Protobuf >= 3.30.2 for its SentencePiece model parsing).
         Everything else in the list is Microsoft- or CNCF-published.

         The Protobuf edge is worth naming rather than burying. CBIX uses no tokenizer and no
         protobuf: they arrive because MAF's workflow package depends on the whole
         Microsoft.Extensions.AI evaluation stack whether or not a consumer evaluates anything. So it
         is unused code on the deployed image - which is not a vulnerability, but it IS attack surface
         and audit surface nobody asked for, and a future advisory against Protobuf will hard-fail
         CI's audit step over a package no CBIX line of code calls. Recorded so that when it happens
         nobody spends an afternoon working out why we depend on Protobuf at all.

         None of it is a model-provider SDK, which is the property that actually matters here, since
         Core is the assembly every other project depends on.

         Note what this does NOT mean: Cbix.Core emits a reference to only three of them
         (Microsoft.Agents.AI.Workflows, Microsoft.Agents.AI.Abstractions and
         Microsoft.Extensions.DependencyInjection.Abstractions - plus the ones it already had),
         because a transitively-supplied assembly is compilable-against whether or not anything names
         it. CoreAssemblyNeutralityTests asserts on the emitted reference table, so anything in this
         closure that Core starts NAMING has to be added to that allowlist deliberately - which is
         the control, rather than the package list. -->
    <PackageVersion Include="Microsoft.Agents.AI.Workflows" Version="[1.17.0]" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup Label="Provider adapter (Anthropic) - story S01-08">
    <!--
      Both MAF packages are referenced DIRECTLY, not left to arrive through the Anthropic
      integration, because between them they declare the type the adapter's public signature
      returns - the neutral currency the rest of the solution speaks. A type in our own API
      surface should not arrive by accident.
        - Microsoft.Agents.AI.Abstractions declares AIAgent itself (verified: the S01-08
          scenario asserts on the declaring assembly, and caught the assumption that it lived
          in Microsoft.Agents.AI).
        - Microsoft.Agents.AI declares ChatClientAgent, the concrete type AsAIAgent hands back
          and which the adapter widens to AIAgent.
      Note the version scheme: both core packages are STABLE at 1.17.0 while the Anthropic
      integration below is still prerelease. That maturity gap is exactly the design 11
      caution, and it is why the integration is quarantined in its own assembly.

      Since S01-13 these two are no longer adapter-only. Cbix.Core references
      Microsoft.Agents.AI.Abstractions as well, because the workflow graph and the relocated
      document-bound callable name AIAgent, AgentRunOptions and AgentResponse - all declared
      there (measured against the 1.17.0 XML documentation, not recalled). Core does NOT
      reference Microsoft.Agents.AI: it names nothing from it, and the reference table asserted
      in CoreAssemblyNeutralityTests is what keeps that true. This group's label is kept for the
      history it carries; the packages themselves are neutral framework currency.

      Bracket notation is deliberate here and nowhere else in this file. A bare version in
      NuGet is a MINIMUM, not an exact pin; the entries above accept that because they are
      mature packages on a stable line. Design 11 requires exact pinned versions for MAF
      and its provider integration specifically, and `[x]` is the only notation that
      actually delivers one for a local (unlocked) restore. CI's locked restore would catch
      drift after the fact; the bracket prevents it in the first place.
    -->
    <PackageVersion Include="Microsoft.Agents.AI.Abstractions" Version="[1.17.0]" />
    <PackageVersion Include="Microsoft.Agents.AI" Version="[1.17.0]" />
    <!--
      MAF's first-party Anthropic integration (design 7). PRERELEASE - there is no stable
      line to move to, which is the whole reason design 11 says to pin exactly and expect
      breaking changes on upgrade. This version resolves Microsoft.Agents.AI 1.17.0 and
      the official Anthropic .NET SDK 12.35.1 (pinned directly below).

      Upgrade procedure: bump this pin, check whether the integration's dependency group
      moved the `Anthropic` version and bump that pin to match, then require a green
      golden-set run before shipping (CLAUDE.md quality gate).
    -->
    <PackageVersion Include="Microsoft.Agents.AI.Anthropic" Version="[1.17.0-preview.260804.1]" />
    <!--
      The official Anthropic .NET SDK. Pinned exactly and referenced DIRECTLY by the adapter,
      which is an accurate statement of what the code does: `AsAIAgent` is an extension on
      IAnthropicClient, so the adapter must construct an AnthropicClient and its ClientOptions
      itself. Those SDK types are named in AnthropicAgentFactory today.

      Two things this pin does NOT do, stated plainly because an earlier version of this
      comment got both wrong:
        - It does not weaken containment. Containment was never a property of the package
          graph: a transitively-supplied assembly is fully compilable-against, so any project
          could have named these types whether or not it declared a PackageReference. What
          actually enforces containment is the reference-table assertion in
          ProviderContainmentTests / the S01-08 BDD scenario, which fails if any assembly
          outside the adapter emits a reference to `Anthropic` or
          `Microsoft.Agents.AI.Anthropic`.
        - Conversely, leaving it transitive did not pin the version. The MAF integration
          declares `Anthropic >= 12.35.1`, so a lock regeneration would float it upward
          silently. The exact pin plus CI's locked restore is what holds the version still -
          which matters because the adapter depends on measured SDK behaviour (see the
          ambient-credential remarks on AnthropicProviderOptions).
    -->
    <PackageVersion Include="Anthropic" Version="[12.35.1]" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup Label="Test">
    <PackageVersion Include="coverlet.collector" Version="6.0.4" />
    <PackageVersion Include="Microsoft.NET.Test.Sdk" Version="17.14.1" />
    <PackageVersion Include="xunit" Version="2.9.3" />
    <PackageVersion Include="xunit.runner.visualstudio" Version="3.1.4" />
    <!-- Runtime-conditional skips. xUnit v2 cannot skip from inside a test, so a probe whose
         precondition the host denies (creating a symbolic link needs a Windows privilege) would
         otherwise have to `return` silently and report itself as passed - a security probe that
         lies. Pinned to the version Reqnroll.xUnit already resolves transitively, so the graph
         gains no new resolution. -->
    <PackageVersion Include="Xunit.SkippableFact" Version="1.4.13" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup Label="BDD">
    <!-- Reqnroll.xUnit is the single entry point: it pulls in Reqnroll and
         Reqnroll.Tools.MsBuild.Generation (the .feature -> .feature.cs generator).
         3.3.4 depends on xunit.core 2.8.1, satisfied by our xunit 2.9.3. -->
    <PackageVersion Include="Reqnroll.xUnit" Version="3.3.4" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
